22:03:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Islamic texts, like many ancient religious texts, contain passages about war, punishment, and conflict. Those passages can be cited by extremists—but that does not mean violence is “baked into” the religion in a way that defines all Muslims or inevitably produces terrorism. Interpretation matters: Muslims disagree about how particular verses apply, and mainstream Islamic scholarship does not treat historical wartime passages as a blanket permission to attack civilians or terrorize people. The Qur’an also contains teachings commonly understood to prohibit coercion in religion and to emphasize justice, mercy, and the sanctity of innocent life. The same basic issue arises with other traditions: the Bible and other religious texts include violent material that has sometimes been used to justify real harm. Yet it would be wrong to infer from that that Christians or Jews as groups are inherently violent. Texts can be interpreted, selectively quoted, and weaponized; people and institutions are responsible for what they choose to do with them. It is fair to criticize a specific doctrine, passage, preacher, or movement if you can identify what it says and why you think it is harmful. It is not fair—or accurate—to move from “some passages have been invoked by violent groups” to “Muslims are hateful.” Your neighbor’s actual conduct is much better evidence of their character than an assumption about what a text supposedly compels them to believe.
