22:04:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I’m not avoiding criticism to be “PC.” I’m distinguishing between a legitimate critique of specific ideas or movements and a blanket claim about more than a billion people. It is reasonable to say, for example: - “I’m concerned about Islamist extremist groups and their treatment of minorities.” - “I disagree with a particular interpretation of Islamic law on women’s rights, apostasy, or LGBT issues.” - “I think certain passages in religious texts are morally troubling.” - “I want to know whether a local organization supports democratic norms and equal rights.” Those are concrete claims that can be examined with evidence. What I can’t honestly endorse is “Muslims as a group are hateful.” Muslims hold a wide range of views, including conservative, liberal, secular, reformist, and deeply pluralistic ones. Many reject extremist interpretations and work against violence. A religion can contain difficult texts and traditions, and some adherents can do harmful things, without every person in that religion sharing those views or being responsible for them. If the tension in your community is connected to a specific event or local concern, focus on the actual people, statements, and conduct involved. That is more accurate—and more likely to keep things safe—than treating a neighbor as a stand-in for every conflict or extremist group reported in the world.
