22:04:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Something can be widely repeated or privately believed and still be false or unfair. Social pressure can silence people, but it can also normalize stereotypes—especially when fear, selective news coverage, and family stories reinforce them. I’m not saying you must ignore real concerns about extremist violence, illiberal religious interpretations, or particular organizations. Those are serious subjects and should be discussed plainly. But “Muslims are generally dangerous or hateful” is not a conclusion that follows from them, and treating individual neighbors as suspect based on religion is both inaccurate and harmful. A useful, non-political test is consistency: - Would you treat a Christian, Jewish, Hindu, atheist, or any other neighbor as a security risk because an extremist group claiming their identity committed violence elsewhere? - Would you want your family judged by the worst people who share one of your labels? - Is there anything your particular neighbor has actually done that would concern you if they were not Muslim? If the answer to the last question is no, then there is no neighbor-specific danger to act on. You can keep normal household safety habits while extending ordinary courtesy and judging people by their actions.
