22:06:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You can trust your eyes about what you directly saw: who said or did what, where, and under what circumstances. The part worth checking is the broader conclusion drawn from those experiences. For example: - “I saw this person express hatred” may be a solid observation. - “People in this particular group or setting often expressed that view” may be a reasonable concern to investigate. - “Therefore a Muslim neighbor I do not know is likely hateful or dangerous” goes beyond what those observations alone establish. That is not a failure of judgment; it is a normal human reasoning challenge. Everyone—including experts—can overgeneralize from vivid cases, family stories, and selective information. Good judgment means separating observation from inference, seeking disconfirming evidence, and updating conclusions when the evidence warrants it. You can remain practical: use ordinary safety precautions, notice concrete behavior, and set boundaries with anyone who acts threatening or disrespectful. At the same time, give your neighbor the basic presumption that they are responsible for their own choices, not for the actions of strangers who share a religion.
