22:07:09 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A map can show a real geographic pattern without identifying a single cause. Many conflict-affected Muslim-majority regions have also experienced colonial borders, proxy wars, foreign interventions, authoritarian rule, ethnic and sectarian competition, resource politics, displacement, and state collapse. Those factors can drive violence regardless of religion—and violent groups often use religion as one source of legitimacy in those circumstances. There are also many Muslim-majority countries with low levels of violence, and many severe conflicts in non-Muslim-majority places. So “some conflict zones are Muslim-majority” does not establish that Islam itself is the cause. It is fair to examine whether particular religious interpretations worsen a particular conflict. But that requires looking at the actual actors and mechanisms: What are they fighting over? Who is financing them? What do leaders teach? Are civilians being targeted? What local political conditions make recruitment possible? None of that gives a sound basis to infer that your particular neighbor is dangerous. A country-level conflict statistic cannot tell you much about the character or intentions of one person living next door.
