22:08:37 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes—being honest about real problems is better than pretending they do not exist. The important part is being precise about what the problem is. It is reasonable to openly address things like: - extremist groups that use Islamic language to justify violence; - anti-Jewish, anti-Christian, anti-Muslim, sexist, or anti-LGBT hatred expressed by particular people or organizations; - coercion, abuse, or discrimination in any community; - harmful teachings or policies, with specific evidence and context. That kind of honesty allows people—including Muslims who oppose those things—to challenge them. What does not help is treating “Muslims” as the problem. That makes ordinary people defensive or vulnerable, obscures who is actually responsible, and can push community discussions toward suspicion rather than solutions. A more constructive way to put it is: “What specific belief, practice, or organization are we concerned about, and what evidence do we have?”
